(  111. BIG O DEFINITION) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  1. FORMULA: f\(n\) = O\(g\(n\)\) if there exist constants c,n0 such that f\(n\) <= c*g\(n\) for all n >= n0.) Tj
T*
(  2. CONCEPT: Upper-bound growth rate for large inputs.) Tj
T*
(  3. INTUITION: Ignore constant factors and smaller terms because dominant growth controls scalability.) Tj
T*
(  4. DSA CONNECTION: All algorithm analysis.) Tj
T*
(  5. INTERVIEW PROBLEMS: Every interview problem discussion.) Tj
T*
(  6. PATTERN RECOGNITION: Need to explain runtime/space as input grows.) Tj
T*
(  7. VISUALIZATION: 3n^2 + 10n + 5 behaves like n^2 for large n.) Tj
T*
(  8. COMMON MISTAKES: Calling O\(n\) "exact time"; Big O is an upper bound.) Tj
T*
(  9. MEMORY TRICK: "Keep the fastest-growing term.") Tj
T*
(  10. IMPLEMENTATION IMPACT: Helps choose algorithms that fit constraints.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  112. COMMON GROWTH ORDER) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  1. FORMULA: O\(1\) < O\(log n\) < O\(n\) < O\(n log n\) < O\(n^2\) < O\(2^n\) < O\(n!\).) Tj
T*
(  2. CONCEPT: Standard scalability hierarchy.) Tj
T*
(  3. INTUITION: Polynomial, exponential, and factorial growth separate dramatically as n increases.) Tj
T*
(  4. DSA CONNECTION: Complexity analysis, algorithm selection.) Tj
T*
(  5. INTERVIEW PROBLEMS: All performance discussions.) Tj
T*
(  6. PATTERN RECOGNITION: Compare constraints to feasible growth.) Tj
T*
(  7. VISUALIZATION: For n=1000, n^2=1,000,000, but 2^n is impossible.) Tj
T*
(  8. COMMON MISTAKES: Thinking O\(n log n\) and O\(n^2\) are close for large n.) Tj
T*
(  9. MEMORY TRICK: "Constant, log, linear, sort, square, subset, permutation.") Tj
T*
(  10. IMPLEMENTATION IMPACT: Guides whether sorting, DP, or pruning is acceptable.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  113. LOGARITHMIC GROWTH) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  1. FORMULA: Repeated division by b: iterations ceil\(log_b n\).) Tj
T*
(  2. CONCEPT: Logarithms count how many times input shrinks by a constant factor.) Tj
T*
(  3. INTUITION: After t steps, size is n/b^t; solve n/b^t <= 1.) Tj
T*
