(  118. AMORTIZED ANALYSIS) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  1. FORMULA: Amortized cost = total cost over sequence / number of operations.) Tj
T*
(  2. CONCEPT: Occasional expensive operations can average out over many cheap ones.) Tj
T*
(  3. INTUITION: Expensive resize/rebuild is paid for by many previous simple operations.) Tj
T*
(  4. DSA CONNECTION: Dynamic arrays, hash tables, stacks, queues, DSU.) Tj
T*
(  5. INTERVIEW PROBLEMS: Min Stack \(LC 155\), Implement Queue using Stacks \(LC 232\), RandomizedSet \(LC) Tj
T*
(    380\).) Tj
T*
(  6. PATTERN RECOGNITION: A data structure occasionally resizes, rebuilds, or compresses paths.) Tj
T*
(  7. VISUALIZATION: Dynamic array doubles capacity; copying after sizes 1,2,4,8 sums below 2n.) Tj
T*
(  8. COMMON MISTAKES: Confusing amortized O\(1\) with worst-case O\(1\).) Tj
T*
(  9. MEMORY TRICK: "Spread the spike over the sequence.") Tj
T*
(  10. IMPLEMENTATION IMPACT: Justifies practical O\(1\) append/hash operations.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  119. SORTING LOWER BOUND) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  1. FORMULA: Comparison sorting needs Omega\(n log n\) comparisons in the worst case.) Tj
T*
(  2. CONCEPT: Any comparison sort must distinguish among n! possible orders.) Tj
T*
(  3. INTUITION: A comparison decision tree with height h has at most 2^h leaves; need 2^h >= n!.) Tj
T*
(  4. DSA CONNECTION: Sorting, selection, lower-bound reasoning.) Tj
T*
(  5. INTERVIEW PROBLEMS: Sort an Array \(LC 912\), Kth Largest Element \(LC 215\).) Tj
T*
(  6. PATTERN RECOGNITION: If only comparisons are allowed, do not expect better than O\(n log n\) full) Tj
T*
(    sort.) Tj
T*
(  7. VISUALIZATION: Every possible permutation must end at a different leaf in the comparison tree.) Tj
T*
(  8. COMMON MISTAKES: Sorting when selection/top-k would solve in O\(n\) or O\(n log k\).) Tj
T*
(  9. MEMORY TRICK: "Sorting must identify one of n! orders.") Tj
T*
(  10. IMPLEMENTATION IMPACT: Motivates heap/quickselect/counting sort when full comparison sorting is) Tj
T*
(    unnecessary or keys are bounded.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  120. CONSTRAINT-TO-COMPLEXITY HEURISTIC) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(  1. FORMULA: Rough feasible operations: 10^8 simple operations per second in compiled languages;) Tj
T*
(    interview Java usually aim lower.) Tj
T*
(  2. CONCEPT: Input size suggests acceptable complexity.) Tj
T*
(  3. INTUITION: Algorithms must fit both asymptotic growth and constant factors.) Tj
T*
(  4. DSA CONNECTION: Choosing between brute force, DP, sorting, greedy, graph algorithms.) Tj
T*
(  5. INTERVIEW PROBLEMS: All timed coding assessments.) Tj
T*
ET
